\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{A nullcline meets the orbits at an equilibrium}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The [nullclines](fnip-0014) locate the [equilibria](fnip-0014).
The [phase portrait](fnip-0015) is the partition into
[orbits](fnip-0015).}

\p{For the Morris–Lecar velocity of \ref{fnip-0005}, the second
component vanishes on the graph #{n=n_{\infty}(V)}. Differentiating
the formula for #{n_{\infty}} in that example gives
##{\frac{d n_{\infty}}{d V}
   =\frac{1}{2V_{4}\cosh^{2}((V-V_{3})/V_{4})}.}
In the Hopf column #{V_{4}=30}, so this derivative is positive at
every #{V}. At a point of the graph other than an
[equilibrium](fnip-0014), the velocity is horizontal and nonzero, so
its slope in the #{(V,n)} plane is #{0}. The slope of the graph is
positive. If a solution #{(V(t),n(t))} starts at such a point, the
derivative of #{n(t)-n_{\infty}(V(t))} equals
#{-(dn_{\infty}/dV)\,\dot V}, which is nonzero. The solution leaves
the graph. The only [orbit](fnip-0015) that remains on the graph is the
equilibrium.}
